  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Expression for modulation is given by,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>s</mi><mfenced><mi>t</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>cos</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced><mi>cos</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;are the frequency of the modulating signal and the carrier signal respectively.</p><p>Comparing it with the given expression in question, we get,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi></math></p><p>Now, Bandwidth&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kHz</mi></math></p>",
